\begin{problem}
	Let $U \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be bounded and $u \in C^2\left(U_T\right) \cap C\left(\bar{U}_T\right)$ a subsolution of the heat equation
\[
\Delta u-u_t \geq 0 \quad \text { in } U_T .
\]

Use the following steps to give a direct proof of the maximum principle
\[
\max _{\bar{U}_T} u=\max _{\Gamma_T} u
\]
(Here $\left.U_T=U \times(0, T], \Gamma_T=\bar{U}_T \backslash U_T\right)$.\\
(a) Assume first that $u$ is a strict subsolution, i.e., $\Delta u-u_t>0$ in $U_T$ and $\left(x_0, t_0\right) \in \bar{U}_T$ be such that
\[
u\left(x_0, t_0\right)=\max _{\bar{U}_T} u
\]

Show that $\left(x_0, t_0\right) \notin U_T$ and consequently maximum principle holds for $u$.
[Hint: Show that if $\left(x_0, t_0\right) \in U_T$, then $u_{x_i x_i}\left(x_0, t_0\right) \leq 0, i=1, \ldots, n$ and $u_t\left(x_0, t_0\right) \geq 0$ ]\\
(b) Apply (a) to $u_\epsilon(x, t)=u(x, t)-\epsilon t$ for a small $\epsilon>0$ and let $\epsilon \rightarrow 0$ to obtain the maximum principle for all (non necessarily strict) subsolutions $u$.
\end{problem}
\begin{proof}
	Assume $u$ is strict subsolution.
	Let $(x_0, t_0) \in U_T$. Let $\mathbb{e}$ be any unit vector lying in $\mathbb{R}^n$, and consider the set $I := (x_0 - \delta \mathbb{e}, x_0 + \delta \mathbb{e}) \times \{t_0\} $, $\delta>0$ small enough so that $I \subset U_T$. Assume $u$ is strict subsolution, and assume $u$ attains maximum at $(x_0, t_0)$. We can apply second derivative test to $u|_I$ and conclude that $D_{\mathbb{e}}^2 u (x_0, t_0) \le 0$. Hence $u_{x_i x_i }(x_0, t_0) \le 0$.

	Now take $\delta>0$ such that $[t_0 - \delta, t_0] \subset [0,T)$. Restrict $u$ to $J = \{x_0\} \times [t_0-\delta, t_0]$. If $u_t(x_0, t_0) < 0$, there must exist some $(x_0, t') \in J$ such that $u(x_0, t') > u(x_0, t_0)$, a contradiction. Hence $u_t(x_0, t_0) \ge 0$. 

	Combining these two results, we have a contradiction to the assumption $\Delta u - u_t \ge 0$. Hence  $(x_0, t_0) \in \partial_p U_T$.

	Now, assume instead that $u$ is subsolution, not necessarily strictly. Then $u - \varepsilon t$ is a strict subsolution. For each $\varepsilon>0$ we have
	\[
		\max_{\Gamma} u - \varepsilon t = \max_{\overline{U_T} } u - \varepsilon t
	.\] 
	This implies
	\[
		\max_{\overline{U_T} } u 
		\ge  \max_{\Gamma} u \ge \max_{\overline{U_T} } u - \varepsilon T
	.\] 
	Take $\varepsilon > 0$, we get
	\[
		\max_{\Gamma} u = \max_{\overline{U_T} } u 
	.\] 
\end{proof}

\begin{problem}
	Let $u \in C^2\left(\mathbb{R}^n \times(0, T]\right) \cap C\left(\mathbb{R}^n \times[0, T]\right), T>0$ be such that
\[
\Delta u-u_t \leq 0 \quad \text { in } \mathbb{R}^n \times(0, T], \quad u(\cdot, 0) \geq 0 \quad \text { on } \mathbb{R}^n
\]
and
\[
\liminf _{|x| \rightarrow \infty} u(x, t) \geq 0 \quad \text { uniformly in } t \in[0, T] .
\]

Prove that $u(x, t) \geq 0$ in $\mathbb{R}^n \times[0, T]$.
\end{problem}
\begin{proof}
	By assumption, there exists a ball $B_R \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ centered at origin with radius $R$, such that
	\[
		u(x, t) \ge 0, \qquad |x| > R, t \in [0,T]
	.\] 
	Denote $U_T = B_{R+1} \times [0,T)$.
	Now since  $u$ is supercaloric, we have minimum principle
	\[
		\min_{\overline{U_T} } u = \min_{\partial_p U_T} u
	.\] 
	But under assumption that $u(\cdot , 0) \ge 0$ and $u(x,t) \ge 0$ for $|x| > R$, we have
	\[
		\min_{\partial_p U_T} u \ge 0
	.\] 
	Therefore $u \ge 0$ in $B_{R+1} \times [0,T]$. Take $R\to \infty $ and we are done.
\end{proof}

